::ENG2_U03_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Prompt: Should the city repair cracked sidewalks before planting new decorative trees downtown? Evidence: Cracked sidewalks have caused several reported falls. Decorative trees could make the area more attractive. Question: Which claim is strongest?{
~A. Sidewalks and trees are both downtown.#Incorrect. This states a topic but does not make an argument.
=B. The city should repair cracked sidewalks first because the reported falls show an immediate safety concern.#Correct. The claim takes a clear position and supports it with relevant evidence.
~C. Trees are pretty and sidewalks are boring.#Incorrect. This is informal and unsupported.
~D. The city has many choices.#Incorrect. This is too general to function as an argument claim.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Claim: The city should plant new decorative trees first. Evidence options: 1. Decorative trees could make the area more attractive to visitors. 2. Cracked sidewalks have caused several reported falls. Question: Which evidence best supports the claim?{
~A. Cracked sidewalks have caused falls.#Incorrect. This supports repairing sidewalks, not planting trees.
~B. Sidewalks are made of concrete.#Incorrect. This does not support either priority.
~C. The city has a downtown area.#Incorrect. This is background information, not evidence for the claim.
=D. Decorative trees could make the area more attractive to visitors.#Correct. This evidence directly supports the claim about planting trees first.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Counterclaim: Some residents argue that decorative trees should come first because they could attract more visitors. Question: Which rebuttal most directly answers this counterclaim?{
=A. Although trees may attract visitors, repairing cracked sidewalks should come first because reported falls show a safety problem.#Correct. This fairly acknowledges the counterclaim and responds with stronger evidence.
~B. Trees have leaves and branches.#Incorrect. This does not respond to the argument about visitors.
~C. Visitors should ignore sidewalks.#Incorrect. This is not a logical or evidence-based response.
~D. The downtown area exists.#Incorrect. This does not answer the counterclaim.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Draft: "Only clueless people would care about decorative trees when sidewalks are dangerous." Question: Which revision best improves objective tone?{
~A. People who like trees are completely wrong.#Incorrect. This keeps biased language.
~B. Trees are dumb because sidewalks are dangerous.#Incorrect. This remains informal and biased.
=C. Although decorative trees may improve the downtown appearance, sidewalk repair should come first because cracked pavement has caused reported falls.#Correct. This revision uses formal, evidence-based, objective language.
~D. The sidewalk thing is bad and trees are whatever.#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Draft: "The city should repair sidewalks first because cracked pavement has caused several reported falls. This matters because public walkways should be safe for daily use." Question: What is the second sentence doing?{
=A. It explains the reasoning that connects evidence to the claim.#Correct. The sentence explains why the evidence about falls matters.
~B. It removes the evidence.#Incorrect. It explains the evidence rather than removing it.
~C. It states an unrelated counterclaim.#Incorrect. The sentence supports the claim instead of opposing it.
~D. It gives a title for the source.#Incorrect. It is not a source title.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Draft sentence: "The city should repair sidewalks first they have caused falls." Question: Which revision is clearest and most correct?{
~A. The city should repair sidewalks first they have caused falls and.#Incorrect. This is still grammatically incomplete.
~B. Repair sidewalks first falls caused.#Incorrect. This is not a complete sentence.
~C. The city should repair first, sidewalks, because sidewalks, falls.#Incorrect. This punctuation does not create a clear sentence.
=D. The city should repair sidewalks first because cracked sidewalks have caused falls.#Correct. This revision clearly connects the claim and reason.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Prompt: Should a library extend evening hours before buying new display screens? Evidence: Evening hours would help students who work after school. Display screens could advertise events. Question: Which sentence is a fair counterclaim?{
~A. Display screens are useless and nobody needs them.#Incorrect. This is biased and unfair.
=B. Some people may argue that display screens should come first because they could advertise library events.#Correct. This fairly states the opposing position and reason.
~C. The library should extend evening hours.#Incorrect. This restates the claim, not the opposing view.
~D. Students sometimes work after school.#Incorrect. This supports evening hours, not the counterclaim.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Claim: The library should extend evening hours before buying display screens. Evidence: Evening hours would help students who work after school. Question: Which reasoning best explains the evidence?{
~A. Screens can be mounted on walls.#Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence about student access.
~B. Libraries often have books.#Incorrect. This is too general and unrelated.
=C. Students who work after school need later access times so they can still use library resources.#Correct. This reasoning explains why evening hours support the claim.
~D. Events can be advertised in many ways.#Incorrect. This may relate to screens, but it does not explain the evidence for evening hours.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Draft: "The library should extend hours because students who work after school need later access. Screens could advertise events, but access to resources is the more immediate need." Question: Which description best explains the organization?{
~A. The counterclaim comes first and the claim never appears.#Incorrect. The first sentence states the claim.
~B. The paragraph is only a list of unrelated facts.#Incorrect. The sentences are connected by an argument.
~C. The draft uses only emotional language.#Incorrect. The draft uses evidence-based reasoning.
=D. Claim and evidence come first, followed by a counterclaim and rebuttal.#Correct. The draft makes the claim, supports it, acknowledges screens, and explains why access is stronger.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Opposing argument: Display screens should come first because they could advertise library events. Which question best tests the validity of this argument?{
=A. Is there evidence that event attendance is currently low because advertising is weak?#Correct. This question checks whether the reason actually supports the priority.
~B. What color are the display screens?#Incorrect. Color does not test the strength of the reasoning.
~C. How many students have library cards?#Incorrect. This may be useful context, but it does not directly test the advertising claim.
~D. Do display screens ever use electricity?#Incorrect. This does not test whether they should come first.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Prompt: Should the school add a bus route before building an outdoor classroom? Evidence: The bus route would help students who currently walk along a road without sidewalks. An outdoor classroom could support science lessons. Question: Which rebuttal is strongest?{
~A. Outdoor classrooms are silly.#Incorrect. This is biased and does not use evidence.
~B. Buses are vehicles.#Incorrect. This fact does not answer the counterclaim.
=C. Although an outdoor classroom could support science lessons, the bus route should come first because it addresses student transportation safety.#Correct. This acknowledges the opposing idea and responds with stronger safety-based reasoning.
~D. Science lessons can happen anywhere, so no one should care.#Incorrect. This is dismissive and unsupported.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence best uses objective tone?{
~A. The outdoor classroom idea is a total waste.#Incorrect. This uses biased language.
=B. The bus route should be prioritized because students currently walk along a road without sidewalks.#Correct. This sentence uses formal wording and evidence.
~C. The bus route is obviously the only smart idea.#Incorrect. "Obviously" creates unsupported certainty.
~D. Anybody who disagrees is wrong.#Incorrect. This attacks people instead of using reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Argument paragraph order: claim, evidence, reasoning, counterclaim, rebuttal. Which sentence should appear last?{
=A. However, transportation safety should come first because students face the road condition every school day.#Correct. This is the rebuttal because it responds to the counterclaim and returns to the claim.
~B. The school should add a bus route before building an outdoor classroom.#Incorrect. This is the claim and should appear early.
~C. The bus route would help students who currently walk along a road without sidewalks.#Incorrect. This is evidence for the claim.
~D. Some people may prefer an outdoor classroom because it could support science lessons.#Incorrect. This is the counterclaim.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Draft: "Some people may prefer the outdoor classroom, however the bus route addresses safety." Question: Which revision uses punctuation correctly?{
~A. Some people may prefer the outdoor classroom however the bus route addresses safety.#Incorrect. The sentence needs punctuation around "however."
~B. Some people may prefer, the outdoor classroom however, the bus route addresses safety.#Incorrect. The commas interrupt the sentence incorrectly.
~C. Some people may prefer the outdoor classroom however, because the bus route.#Incorrect. This creates an incomplete and unclear sentence.
=D. Some people may prefer the outdoor classroom; however, the bus route addresses safety.#Correct. The semicolon and comma correctly connect two closely related complete thoughts.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.V.01] In an argument, what does "rebuttal" mean?{
~A. A sentence that changes the topic.#Incorrect. A rebuttal stays focused on the argument.
~B. A detail that describes the setting.#Incorrect. That is not the role of a rebuttal in argument writing.
=C. A response that explains why the counterclaim is less convincing than the claim.#Correct. A rebuttal answers the opposing view with reasoning or evidence.
~D. A word that makes writing sound informal.#Incorrect. Rebuttal is an academic argument term, not informal language.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Claim: The town should repair the river trail before adding festival lights. Evidence: Broken pavement creates safety risks for walkers and cyclists. Question: Which sentence best explains the evidence?{
~A. Festival lights can be bright.#Incorrect. This does not explain the safety evidence.
=B. Walkers and cyclists use the trail, so broken pavement can affect their daily safety.#Correct. This reasoning connects the evidence to the claim.
~C. Trails are outside.#Incorrect. This is a general fact, not reasoning.
~D. Some festivals happen at night.#Incorrect. This relates to lights, not trail safety.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Counterclaim: Festival lights should come first because they could attract visitors. Which evidence would best support that counterclaim?{
~A. Broken pavement creates safety risks for walkers and cyclists.#Incorrect. This supports repairing the trail.
~B. Some walkers use bicycles.#Incorrect. This does not support festival lights.
=C. A nearby town increased evening visitors after adding lights to its festival area.#Correct. This evidence directly supports the idea that lights can attract visitors.
~D. The river trail is outdoors.#Incorrect. This is background information, not support for the counterclaim.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best removes biased language from this sentence: "Only careless leaders would choose festival lights first."{
=A. Choosing festival lights first may be less urgent because trail damage creates safety risks.#Correct. This revision removes the personal attack and focuses on evidence.
~B. Only very careless leaders would choose festival lights first.#Incorrect. This still attacks people instead of using evidence.
~C. Festival lights are the worst idea ever.#Incorrect. This remains biased and exaggerated.
~D. Leaders who like lights do not understand anything.#Incorrect. This is an unsupported personal attack.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which sentence combines claim, evidence, and reasoning most effectively?{
~A. The town should repair the trail first.#Incorrect. This is a claim, but it lacks evidence and reasoning.
=B. The town should repair the trail first because broken pavement creates safety risks for walkers and cyclists, making daily use of the trail less safe.#Correct. This includes claim, evidence, and reasoning in one clear sentence.
~C. Broken pavement exists on the river trail.#Incorrect. This is evidence only.
~D. Festival lights and trails are both in town.#Incorrect. This does not form a supported argument.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Draft: "Although festival lights could attract visitors. Repairing the trail should come first." Question: Which revision fixes the fragment?{
~A. Although festival lights could attract visitors repairing the trail should come first.#Incorrect. The sentence needs a comma after the introductory clause.
~B. Although festival lights. Could attract visitors repairing the trail.#Incorrect. This creates more fragments.
~C. Repairing the trail although festival lights could.#Incorrect. This is not a complete logical sentence.
=D. Although festival lights could attract visitors, repairing the trail should come first.#Correct. This revision completes the thought and uses correct punctuation.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Prompt: Should a school replace broken hallway lockers before purchasing new courtyard benches? Evidence: Broken lockers do not close securely. Courtyard benches would give students more outdoor seating. Which counterclaim is fair and relevant?{
=A. Some students may argue that courtyard benches should come first because they would provide more outdoor seating.#Correct. This fairly states the opposing position and reason.
~B. Broken lockers do not close securely.#Incorrect. This supports the claim, not the counterclaim.
~C. Lockers are metal and benches are outside.#Incorrect. This does not present an opposing argument.
~D. Benches are pointless and nobody should want them.#Incorrect. This is biased and unfair.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Claim: The school should replace broken hallway lockers first. Evidence: Broken lockers do not close securely. Question: Which rebuttal best responds to the bench counterclaim?{
~A. Benches are seats.#Incorrect. This does not respond with reasoning.
~B. Outdoor seating is not a thing.#Incorrect. This is inaccurate and dismissive.
=C. Although benches would add outdoor seating, lockers should come first because students need secure storage during the school day.#Correct. This acknowledges the counterclaim and explains why the original claim is stronger.
~D. Lockers have doors.#Incorrect. This is a detail, but it does not explain priority.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence should be removed from a formal argument paragraph because it is least objective?{
~A. Broken lockers do not close securely.#Incorrect. This is objective evidence.
~B. Courtyard benches would give students more outdoor seating.#Incorrect. This fairly states the opposing evidence.
~C. Secure storage is important during the school day.#Incorrect. This is formal reasoning.
=D. Anyone who wants benches first is not thinking clearly.#Correct. This sentence attacks people instead of using objective reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.R.08] Which sentence best shows an argument writer understands shared information between two sides?{
~A. Only one side has any reason at all.#Incorrect. Shared information recognizes overlap or common ground.
=B. Both sides want to improve the school, but they disagree about which need should come first.#Correct. This identifies common ground while explaining the disagreement.
~C. One side should be ignored completely.#Incorrect. This dismisses the opposing view instead of analyzing it.
~D. Arguments never have anything in common.#Incorrect. Many arguments include shared goals or concerns.
}

::ENG2_U03_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.W.02] Which checklist best matches a strong formal argument paragraph for this lesson?{
=A. Clear claim, relevant evidence, reasoning, fair counterclaim, evidence-based rebuttal, objective tone.#Correct. These are the key features practiced in Lesson 7.
~B. Counterclaim only, no response, informal language.#Incorrect. A formal argument must respond to the counterclaim and use objective tone.
~C. Topic only, personal insult, no evidence, emotional ending.#Incorrect. This does not meet formal argument expectations.
~D. Evidence only, no claim, no reasoning, no organization.#Incorrect. Evidence must be connected to a claim with reasoning.
}
